  summary: "European interest in Africa revived between the 1780s and 1870s, when explorers entered the interior. Some claimed scientific enquiry, but their backers wanted trading profit and the explorers generally served colonial expansion. Portuguese Catholic missions of the late fifteenth century failed because they carried political aims and challenged rulers' authority, though a convert became king in Kongo in 1506. Jesuit missionaries were expelled from Ethiopia in the mid-seventeenth century. In the nineteenth century British and other missionary societies spread across Africa and often helped prepare the way for colonialism.",
